NAME
       rm - remove files and directories

SYNOPSIS
       rm [-i | -I | -f] [-r] [-d] [-v] FILE...

DESCRIPTION
       rm deletes files. There is no wastebin: removed files are gone. It
       removes the directory entry; the data is freed when no other hard
       link and no open program uses the file.

       Directories are removed only with -r (everything inside them too) or
       -d (empty ones). For root, RHEL defines an alias rm='rm -i', so root
       is asked before each removal; -f overrides it.

       rm refuses to remove / unless told --no-preserve-root.

OPTIONS
       -r, -R, --recursive
              Remove directories and their contents.

       -f, --force
              Never ask, and do not complain about files that do not exist.

       -i     Ask before every removal.

       -I     Ask once before removing more than three files or recursing.

       -d, --dir
              Remove empty directories.

       -v, --verbose
              Print each removal.

EXAMPLES
       Remove a file:

           rm /tmp/notes.txt

       Remove a directory tree without questions:

           rm -rf /tmp/build

       Remove a file whose name begins with a dash:

           rm -- -oops.txt

SEE ALSO
       rmdir(1), unlink(1), shred(1), find(1)
